# tunnel-puck settings for one gateway a puck serves (a WireGuard gateway: the
# server your clients connect to, on a VPS or other machine you control):
# configs/<puck>.<gateway>.conf (git ignores that name). scripts/tpk-ctl.sh <puck>
# gateway add <gateway> makes it: for a wgt-ctl server of that name it fills in
# TPK_ROUTING="wgt" and WGT_SERVER and carries on; for any other name it stops so
# you can fill in the tpk block. <gateway> is a-z, 0-9 and -, starting with a
# letter (not "wifi"). Shell syntax: NAME="value".
# Commands for this gateway alone: tpk-ctl.sh <puck>.<gateway> <command>.

# What sends clients into the puck:
#   tpk: tunnel-puck's own rules, on any WireGuard gateway. Fill in the tpk block.
#   wgt: the gateway runs wgt (the wireguard-tools in this repository); the
#        puck becomes a wgt jump, and the gateway is driven through wgt-ctl. Fill
#        in the wgt block.
TPK_ROUTING="tpk"

PUCK_PORT=""                        # the link's UDP port on the gateway. Empty: link picks a free one (from 51821) and saves it here. 443, if free, is rarely filtered
# The link's subnet, a /30 in 10.99.0.0/24. Leave it empty: link picks one that's
# free on the gateway and among the puck's other links, and saves it here. The
# puck's interfaces for this gateway are named from it (the n-th /30: wgpk<n>,
# wgrtun<n>). Never change it once set.
TPK_LINK=""

# TPK_ROUTING="tpk" only
GATEWAY_SSH="root@gateway.example.com"  # how ssh reaches the gateway: root, or a user with passwordless sudo
GATEWAY_HOST="gateway.example.com"      # the gateway's public name or IP, as the puck dials it
CLIENT_IPS=""                       # the clients that exit via the puck: space-separated IPv4 addresses, as in each client's config
CLIENT_IP6S=""                      # their IPv6 addresses, if any (blocked: the link is IPv4 only)
WG_IF="wg0"                         # the WireGuard interface those clients connect to on the gateway
WG_SUBNET=""                        # leave empty (it means every subnet WG_IF is on)

# TPK_ROUTING="wgt" only
# The gateway's name in wgt-ctl's list (`bin/wgt-ctl list`). Its ssh target and
# address come from that list. Empty: this file's <gateway>, if wgt-ctl lists it,
# else tpk-ctl.sh asks, offering the listed servers or a new one (bin/wgt-ctl
# init), and saves the answer here.
WGT_SERVER=""
# Jump codes are wgt's: 2-6 of a-z 0-9 -. Empty, they're made from the puck's
# name, shortened to fit (grandma: grandm, LAN gran-l); tpk-ctl.sh config shows
# them. A code the gateway already uses for something else is refused. The same
# code can be used on each gateway: codes are per server.
JUMP_CODE=""                        # the puck's jump on the gateway; empty: the puck's name (6 at most)
JUMP_LABEL=""                       # a label for it, e.g. "Friend's house"; empty: the puck's name
JUMP_LAN=""                         # yes: a second jump in LAN mode (2.3), its code the jump's with -l (mom: mom-l)
JUMP_LAN_CODE=""                    # or name the LAN jump yourself (this also turns it on)
JUMP_LAN_LABEL=""                   # empty: the jump's label with " (LAN)"
